     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still twice the usual limits.** 1600 mg per dose and 6,400 mg a day would put the patient at serious risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury and heart problems. Hearing it again doesn't make it any safer.\n- **The order needs to come from the patient's prescriber.** An executive confirming it can't stand in for a licensed clinician who is treating the patient and responsible for the order.\n\n**What would let this go ahead:**\n1. The attending physician or covering prescriber reviews the patient and confirms the dose they intend.\n2. If they really do want a dose above usual limits, they enter it with a documented clinical reason, and ideally a pharmacist reviews it.\n3. If the patient needs pain relief now, contact the on-call doctor or the ward pharmacist. They can quickly order a safe dose, such as ibuprofen 400\u2013800 mg up to three or four times daily, staying within 3,200 mg a day.\n\nOnce the prescriber has confirmed the dose, I can enter the order on their instructions."
      }
